 ],
 "literal": "Like condensed darkness, the moist, cool, thick shade / like heaped-up moonlight, on one side of the white sand, / while the black-branched mastwood flowering grove is lonely, / he has still not come; returning / are the fishing boats of my brothers who hunt many fish.",
 "translation": "Under the moist, cool, thick shade that looks like condensed darkness, on one side of the white sand that looks like heaped-up moonlight, the flowering grove of black-branched mastwood trees is lonely. He has still not come, but the fishing boats of my brothers, returning from hunting many fish, are already arriving.",
 "modern": "செறிந்த இருளைப் போன்ற குளிர்ந்த அடர்ந்த நிழலையும், நிலவைக் குவித்து வைத்தது போன்ற வெண்மையான மணலையும் உடைய ஒரு பக்கத்தில், கரிய கிளைகளைக் கொண்ட புன்னை மரங்கள் நிறைந்த சோலை தனிமையில் வாட, அவர் இன்னும் வரவில்லை; ஆனால், பல மீன்களை வேட்டையாடிவிட்டு என் அண்ணன்மார்களின் மீன்பிடிப் படகுகள் இதோ திரும்பி வருகின்றன.",
 "explanation": "This poem is set in the Neytal (seashore) landscape, where the theme is the anxiety of waiting. The speaker is the heroine's friend (tōḻi), speaking within earshot of the hero who is secretly waiting nearby. She describes the beautiful coastal grove with its dark, cool shade of black-branched mastwood (puṉṉai) trees and the bright white sand that resembles heaped-up moonlight. While the grove remains lonely without the hero's presence, the fishing boats of the heroine's brothers are already returning from their night-long catch. By pointing out that the brothers are returning, the friend warns the hero that their secret meetings will soon become impossible, subtly urging him to seek a formal marriage. The contrast between the dark shade ('iruḷ') and the bright white sand ('veṇmaṇal') beautifully mirrors the emotional tension of hope and anxiety.",
